# ---------- local dev ----------
DATABASE_URL=postgres://localhost:5432/hasino_dev
TEST_DATABASE_URL=postgres://localhost:5432/hasino_test

# Bind port and environment. NODE_ENV=production makes start() refuse to boot
# without real credentials — see src/http/server.ts.
PORT=3000
# NODE_ENV=production

# ---------- Clerk Auth ----------
# Dashboard > API keys. Both come from the same place.
#
# CLERK_PUBLISHABLE_KEY (pk_test_* / pk_live_*) is NOT secret — it is served to
# every browser from GET /api/config, which is how the frontend boots clerk-js.
# CLERK_SECRET_KEY (sk_test_* / sk_live_*) signs and reads on the server and
# must never leave it. Production refuses to boot without both.
CLERK_PUBLISHABLE_KEY=
CLERK_SECRET_KEY=

# In the Clerk dashboard you must also:
#   - User & Authentication > Social Connections: enable Google
#   - User & Authentication > Email, Phone, Username: leave Phone number OFF.
#     Google supplies the whole identity and this app never asks for a number;
#     requiring one in Clerk parks every new sign-up at missing_requirements
#     waiting for input that never comes.
#   - Domains: add http://localhost:3000 for local development

# The Google *Web* OAuth client id, for NATIVE Google sign-in in the Android
# app (Credential Manager). Public (an OAuth client id ships to every client),
# served from GET /api/config. It MUST be the same Web client id you configure
# as Clerk's Google credentials — Clerk verifies the native token against it.
# For native sign-in this also requires:
#   - a Clerk instance using your OWN Google credentials (not Clerk's shared dev
#     ones) with Google One Tap enabled, and
#   - a Google Cloud "Android" OAuth client for com.hasino.app + your signing
#     SHA-1, in the SAME project as the Web client above.
# Unset on web-only deploys; the browser sign-in flow does not use it.
GOOGLE_WEB_CLIENT_ID=

# ---------- location search ----------
# Turning a customer's coordinates into "Sonipat", and a typed city into
# coordinates. Proxied through our own server (src/geo/geocode.ts) so the CSP
# stays same-origin and the provider can be swapped without touching the app.
#
# The default is OpenStreetMap's Nominatim: free, no key, and fine for a pilot.
# Its usage policy asks for an identifying contact and roughly one request a
# second — results are cached for a day, so real traffic is far below that. For
# real volume, self-host Nominatim or point this at Google/Mapbox.
# GEOCODER_URL=https://nominatim.openstreetmap.org
# GEOCODER_CONTACT=you@example.com     # defaults to the first ADMIN_EMAILS entry
# GEOCODER_COUNTRIES=in                # comma separated; limits search results

# ---------- the private admin panel ----------
# The admin panel is a SEPARATE process (`npm run admin`), not a page of the
# public app. The public app serves no admin route, no admin asset and no
# admin API — see src/http/admin-server.ts.
#
# By default it binds to loopback, so the operating system refuses connections
# from any other machine. ADMIN_HOST exists for the rare case of a VPN address
# and has to be typed on purpose; production refuses a routable bind unless the
# panel is deliberately public (below).
ADMIN_PORT=4000
# ADMIN_HOST=127.0.0.1
#
# ADMIN_PUBLIC=true hosts the panel on the internet instead — a second Render
# service, reachable without your laptop. Leave it unset unless you mean it.
#
# What it costs: on loopback, the operating system refuses every outside
# connection and Clerk is a second lock. Public, Clerk and ADMIN_EMAILS are the
# ONLY locks. Every /api/admin/* route still verifies a Clerk token and checks
# the admin role — that has always been true — but nothing else stands in
# front of it any more. ADMIN_EMAILS stops being configuration and becomes a
# credential: anyone who can sign in to Google as one of those addresses
# administers Hasino from anywhere. Put 2FA on those accounts.
#
# Set it and the panel binds 0.0.0.0 on the host's injected PORT. Boot fails
# outright if DEV_AUTH/CI_SMOKE are set, if either Clerk key is missing, or if
# ADMIN_EMAILS is empty — a public panel with a hole in it does not come up.
# ADMIN_PUBLIC=true
#
# Requests are rate limited only when public (the network was the limit
# before). 120/min per caller by default.
# ADMIN_RATE_LIMIT_PER_MIN=120
#
# It reads DATABASE_URL like the public app does — the same production
# database, whether that is over a tunnel from your laptop or from a second
# hosted service. There is no second database and nothing to sync. See
# DEPLOY.md.
#
# No Clerk dashboard change is needed. A development instance accepts any
# localhost origin — there is no Domains list on a dev instance to add one to.
# The panel signs in on its own origin, so a session on the public app does not
# carry over, which is the separation working rather than a problem.

# ---------- who is an admin ----------
# The single source of truth. Comma separated. Re-derived on every sign-in in
# both directions: an address here is promoted to admin, and an admin whose
# address is removed is demoted on their next sign-in. users.role is a cache of
# this, never the other way round — a sticky admin row that outlives its entry
# here is exactly the thing that gets forgotten and then exploited.
#
# Requires a VERIFIED email on the token, so enable Google sign-in (which
# verifies) rather than relying on an unverified claim.
ADMIN_EMAILS=

# ---------- payments ----------
# Which provider takes money. 'none' is a supported, production-legal state,
# not a misconfiguration: Hasino has not chosen a provider yet, and the app
# runs without one. Bookings are taken and hold a real chair, the completion
# OTP still gates service, and the ledger still records what a salon is owed —
# what does not happen is money moving.
#
# Left unset it is derived: 'razorpay' when the Razorpay keys below are present,
# otherwise 'none'. Set it to 'cashfree' to use Cashfree (keys below), or to
# 'none' to stop taking money without deleting a credential.
# PAYMENTS_PROVIDER=cashfree

# ---------- Razorpay ----------
# Dashboard > Account & Settings > API Keys. rzp_test_* against the test mode
# sandbox; rzp_live_* only once the account is activated.
#
# Without these, dev runs against an in-process stub and production refuses to
# boot — a server that takes bookings without taking money is worse than one
# that will not start.
RAZORPAY_KEY_ID=
RAZORPAY_KEY_SECRET=

# ALLOW_UNPAID_BOOKINGS is gone. It was a local-only flag standing between a
# keyless server and an unpaid booking; PAYMENTS_PROVIDER now says the same
# thing in one place, and says it for production too.

# A DIFFERENT secret from the API key: Dashboard > Settings > Webhooks, set when
# you create the endpoint. Point it at https://<your-host>/api/webhooks/razorpay
# and subscribe to: payment.captured, payment.failed, order.paid,
# refund.processed, refund.failed.
#
# This is the backstop for every customer who closes the tab mid-payment. With
# it unset they are debited and get no booking.
RAZORPAY_WEBHOOK_SECRET=

# ---------- Cashfree ----------
# Active when PAYMENTS_PROVIDER=cashfree and the two keys below are set.
# Dashboard > Developers > API Keys. Use the SANDBOX (test) credentials first;
# switch CASHFREE_ENV to production only once the sandbox flow is verified.
#
# The secret key is BOTH the API credential and the webhook signing key, so
# there is no separate webhook secret. Neither key is ever sent to the browser:
# the client opens checkout with a per-order payment_session_id instead.
CASHFREE_APP_ID=
CASHFREE_SECRET_KEY=
# sandbox (default) or production.
CASHFREE_ENV=sandbox
# Optional: pins the Cashfree API version header. Leave unset to use the
# integration's tested default.
# CASHFREE_API_VERSION=2023-08-01
# Optional: the public origin, used to tell Cashfree where to POST webhooks
# (<origin>/api/webhooks/cashfree). Also set the webhook URL in the Cashfree
# dashboard. Subscribe to: PAYMENT_SUCCESS_WEBHOOK, PAYMENT_FAILED_WEBHOOK,
# PAYMENT_USER_DROPPED_WEBHOOK.
# PUBLIC_BASE_URL=https://your-host.example.com

# Platform cut in basis points, applied to new salons at insert time.
# 1500 = 15%. Per-salon overrides live in salons.commission_bps.
PLATFORM_COMMISSION_BPS=1500

# How long a chair is held while the customer pays, in ms. Razorpay checkout
# allows ~5 minutes for a bank/UPI redirect; 8 minutes covers that plus the
# webhook. Shorter frees abandoned chairs sooner but strands slow payers.
PAYMENT_HOLD_TTL_MS=480000

# ---------- notifications ----------
# With no provider set, emails are printed to stdout instead of sent — which is
# what you want locally and never what you want in production.
# RESEND_API_KEY=
# EMAIL_FROM=Hasino <bookings@yourdomain.com>

# ---------- ops ----------
# json (default in production) or pretty (default on a TTY)
# LOG_FORMAT=json
# LOG_LEVEL=info

# Requests per minute per client for the global limit. Booking and auth have
# their own, tighter budgets in src/http/server.ts.
RATE_LIMIT_PER_MIN=300

# Set ONLY when the process actually sits behind a proxy that appends
# X-Forwarded-For. With no proxy in front, trusting that header lets any caller
# forge a fresh client identity per request and bypass rate limiting entirely.
# TRUST_PROXY=true

# Origins allowed to call the API cross-site — for the React Native and Next.js
# clients. Comma separated, exact matches. Empty means same-origin only.
# ALLOWED_ORIGINS=https://app.hasino.in,https://business.hasino.in

# false on a web tier that has a separate worker dyno. The advisory locks in
# src/workers/runner.ts make running them in several processes safe either way.
# RUN_WORKERS=true

# Redis (BullMQ + a shared availability snapshot cache) — not wired yet. The
# cache is in-process today, which is correct for one instance and merely
# wasteful for several.
# REDIS_URL=redis://localhost:6379

# ---------- Android App Links ----------
# The site's half of the agreement that lets the Hasino app catch its own
# OAuth callback instead of leaving the user in Chrome. Served at
# GET /.well-known/assetlinks.json; unset, that route 404s and sign-in on
# Android falls back to finishing in the browser.
#
# The SHA-256 of the certificate the APK you install was signed with, colon
# separated and uppercase. Debug and release builds have different ones — list
# both, comma separated, once there is a release build.
#
#   $ANDROID_HOME/build-tools/36.0.0/apksigner verify --print-certs \
#     android/app/build/outputs/apk/debug/app-debug.apk
#
# ANDROID_CERT_FINGERPRINTS=
# ANDROID_PACKAGE=com.hasino.app     # the default; only set it if you renamed

# ---------- the hosted admin panel, from the customer app ----------
# Where to send a signed-in admin. Only a signpost: this server still has no
# admin route, no admin asset and no /api/admin/*. Unset, an admin stays in the
# customer app and nothing redirects.
#
# For the Android app to open it without handing the user to Chrome, pass the
# same URL as HASINO_ADMIN_URL to `npx cap sync android` so its host joins
# allowNavigation. See README-ANDROID.md.
# ADMIN_PANEL_URL=https://hasino-admin.onrender.com
